uestions that were  percolating in American Studies at the  time about the limits of liberalism and  its promise of freedom through inclusion  right and ways of reckoning with  histories of violence and it's long  persistent aftermaths so soldiering  through Empire which I'm going to talk  about today really grew out of these  conversations it is an attempt to  wrestle with what I see as one of the  central paradoxes of post-1945 u.s.  Imperial culture that is how did the  United States legitimate itself as a  champion of racial equality and  democracy and yet at the same time  expanded state powers to wage war  throughout the decolonizing world how do  we reconcile the seeming contradiction  between these dueling imperatives of  liberal inclusion and racialized state  violence so I approached this question  by turning to the military and exploring  the ways that Asian soldiers were  mobilized for the u.s. for u.s. military  projects in Asia and the Pacific  especially during the Vietnam War in one  sense the book is an attempt to tell a  history of the International Vietnam war  beyond an area studies framework of Cold  War history and to think instead about  the legacies of colonialism in Asia and  the Pacific and how they continue to  manifest through the militarized labor  of their citizens and I look at these  specific sites the Philippines South  Korea and Hawaii in these countries and  territories after World War Two we see a  similar pattern of colonial subjects  being liberated from colonialism so it's  part in u.s. war this process of  ushering freedom in violence was at the  heart of u.s. imperialism after 1945 and  I call this process the making of the  decolonizing Pacific the decolonizing  Pacific is not so much a fixed geography  but rather names a historical  conjuncture in which the unfinished  struggles for decolonization throughout  Asia and the Pacific became intertwined  with and co-opted by the u.s. imperative  of securing the global capitalist